msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:72
#, no-c-format
msgid "Number of possible players:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:72
#, no-c-format
msgid "Four"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:73
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kappname; is a fun and exciting game for &kde; desktop environment. "
"&kappname; is modeled after the well known pen and paper based game of Dots "
"and Boxes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:74
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The idea behind &kappname; is to construct squares by interconnecting the "
"dots on the greed. Up to four players can play this game together, taking "
"turns. The trick is – regardlessly of who has constructed the most of the "
"square, it is the player who completes the square that gets the point for it."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:75
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To win the game round player has to collect the most points by covering the "
"most area with the squares of player's own color."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:78
#, no-c-format
msgid "How to play"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:80
#, no-c-format
msgid "Objective:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:80
#, no-c-format
msgid "Complete more squares than any of your opponents."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:82
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"At the start of the game, the game board is empty. There is simply a grid of "
"dots showing where lines can be drawn."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:85
#, no-c-format
msgid "Note:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:85
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Depending on the game difficulty chosen, the game field may already contain "
"lines at the beginning of the game round."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Each player takes it in turns to draw a line between two adjacent dots on "
"the board. By hovering the mouse over the game board, a yellow indicator "
"line will show you where your line will be placed when you click. Once you "
"have decided where you want to draw your line, click - and the line will be "
"drawn. If by drawing a line, you completed a square then this square now "
"belongs to you and earns you a point. Each time you complete a square, you "
"may draw another line. If your line did not complete a square then the next "
"player(s) take their turn. The game will continue until every square on the "
"board owned by one of the players."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:91
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To start a new game with custom settings (number of players, player names, "
"board size) click on the \"New\" toolbar item or go to "
"<menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action=\"simul\">&Ctrl;<keycap>N</keycap></"
"keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>New</guimenuitem></"
"menuchoice>. To start a new game with the same settings as the current game, "
"click on <guibutton>Reset</guibutton> on the toolbar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:96
#, no-c-format
msgid "Game Rules, Strategies and Tips"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:99
#, no-c-format
msgid "Game Rules"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:100
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There is very little to be said when it come to the rules, as the game "
"concept in itself is rather a simple one. Never the less, here are the game "
"regulations you, as a player will defiantly face while playing &kappname;:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:102
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You may only connect the dots on the greed if they are not previously "
"connected by you, your opponent, or the computer."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:103
#, no-c-format
msgid "To gain the points you have to connect the dots to shape a square."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:104
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The square can be no larger then the four adjacent dots on the greed allow "
"for."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:105
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Once a square is complete the player who draws the last line of that square "
"is granted the right of possession over it. The square then is colored and "
"the player is given a point."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:106
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The player who completes the square is given an extra turn which the player "
"can not forfeit."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:110
#, no-c-format
msgid "Strategies and Tips"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:112
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Try not to play at random. Such game practice will most certainly result in "
"loss."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:113
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Construct long chains of lines over the gameboard. Once one such chain is "
"complete start to build another one going parallel to the first."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:114
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Try no to build contraptions where only one line remains undone. Your "
"opponent will almost certainly take an advantage of it."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:115
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you are to let the opponent get a square or more, try to select the "
"combination resulting in the least damage."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:116
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Try to pus your opponent into the situation where he/she(it) will have no "
"choice but to let you get the square."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:117
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you are to get a chance to start completing the whole chain of squares, "
"remember that by living two squares at the end of such chain incomplete you "
"are pushing your opponent into completing them, thus giving you the "
"opportunity to continue onto yet another chain. Such a situation is called T"
"\\the double-cross."
msgstr ""

